WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.700
The general steps for using an AED on infants and children are exactly the same as they are for adults,

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:10.300
but there is one very important difference to remember, pad placement.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:14.600
For infants and children under the age of eight, we use a front and back placement

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:18.799
to ensure the pads do not touch each other on their smaller chests.

00:00:18.799 --> 00:00:23.400
You will place one pad directly in the center of the chest, between the nipples,

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.200
and the second pad on the back, right between the shoulder blades.

00:00:27.299 --> 00:00:33.500
As always, the best way to be sure is to follow the clear graphics printed right on the pads.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.000
Many AEDs are equipped to handle these younger patients.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.700
Some devices come with a specific set of pediatric pads,

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:46.700
while others have a child mode button or a special key you can insert to reduce the energy of the shock.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:51.400
If the child is eight years old or younger, or weighs less than 55 pounds,

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:56.200
you should always select that child mode or use the pediatric pads if they are available.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:03.900
However, if you are in an emergency and only have adult pads, it is still okay to use them on a child or infant.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:08.300
Just be absolutely certain that the pads do not touch or overlap.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:11.000
One more critical safety rule to remember.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:16.599
While you can use adult pads on a child in a pinch, you should never use pediatric pads on an adult

00:01:16.599 --> 00:01:20.099
because they do not deliver enough energy to be effective.

00:01:20.099 --> 00:01:24.500
Once the pads are on, simply continue to listen to the AED.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:30.800
It will walk you through the analysis and any necessary shocks, just like it did in our adult scenario.

